Welcome to Field Validation and Constraints In Lesson 1 your model checked types: a title had to be a string, a flag had to be a boolean. That&amp;rsquo;s a great first guarantee, but types alone let plenty of nonsense through. An empty string &amp;quot;&amp;quot; is still a valid string. The number -5 is still a valid float. The word &amp;quot;urgent&amp;quot; is still a valid string for priority, even if your app only understands low, medium, and high. Types answer &amp;ldquo;is this the right kind of value?&amp;rdquo; — this lesson answers &amp;ldquo;is this a sensible value?&amp;rdquo;
The tool for that is constraints: extra rules you attach to a field, like &amp;ldquo;at least 1 character&amp;rdquo; or &amp;ldquo;no greater than 100.&amp;rdquo; Pydantic checks them automatically and, when something fails, returns the same clean 422 you already know — now with an error that says exactly which rule was broken. For anything the built-in rules can&amp;rsquo;t express, you&amp;rsquo;ll write your own check with a validator.
